A VLA in a loop body has to give its storage back on the way round, and only
the IR backend does. The legacy backend allocates by moving the stack pointer
and never moves it back before the function returns, so every iteration takes
another slice and this fixture runs it off the end of the stack - exit 139, not
a wrong answer. Its design does not really allow the fix (the allocation is
interleaved with an rsp-based expression stack), and it is not going to get
one, so the fixture is skipped there rather than muted: there is no bug here
waiting to be fixed and flagged.
